        <body>
            <div xml:id="toc">

                <pb facs="0006" n="[II]" xml:id="pgII"/>

                <head>Contents</head>
                <pb facs="0007" n="III" xml:id="pgIII"/>
                <list type="toc">
                    <item>
                        <ref target="#pgVII">Official positions of principal persons mentioned in
                            the correspondence</ref>
                    </item>
                    <item>
                        <ref target="#pgXIII">List of papers by topics, incoming papers
                            chronologically under date of receipt</ref>
                    </item>
                    <item>
                        <ref target="#pgCXLVI">List of papers by countries, incoming papers
                            chronologically under date of issue</ref>
                    </item>
                    <item>PRELIMINARY DOCUMENT <list>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg1">Action of the United States with regard to
                                    violations of the principles of international law by
                                    belligerents</ref>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <item>PART I <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">The Continuation and Further Spread of the
                            War—Efforts Toward Peace</hi>:<list>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg13">Developments in the Balkan countries—Portugal’s
                                    entrance into the war, March 9, 1916—Statements made in
                                    belligerent countries regarding the prospects and terms of
                                    peace</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg28">Spanish proposal for joint good offices, May 11,
                                    1916—Efforts in behalf of peace in European neutral
                                    countries—Attitude of the belligerent countries toward possible
                                    American mediation—Further developments in the Balkan
                                    countries</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg40">Memoranda of the Ambassador in Great Britain of
                                    conversations with British statesmen, July 27-August 1,
                                    1916</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg46">Rejection of the Spanish proposal for joint good
                                    offices, August 23, 1916—Roumania’s entrance into the war,
                                    August 27, 1916—Italy’s declaration of war against Germany,
                                    August 28, 1916</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg55">Germany’s request of September 25, 1916, for the
                                    good offices of the United States—The October revolution in
                                    Greece</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg70">Intimation to Germany, November 29, 1916, of the
                                    President’s willingness to extend good offices if submarine
                                    sinkings and Belgian deportations cease—The contest in
                                    Greece</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg85">The Central powers’ invitation of December 12,
                                    1916, to the Allied powers to discuss peace</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg97">The President’s suggestion of December 18, 1916,
                                    that the belligerent governments communicate their terms of
                                    peace—Lloyd George’s speech of December 19, 1916</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg117">Replies of the Central powers of December 26,
                                    1916, to the President’s suggestion</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg123">Reply of the Allied powers of December 30,
                                    1916, to the invitation of the Central powers to discuss
                                    peace</ref>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <item>PART II <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Neutral Rights</hi>:<list>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg143">Neutral rights as affected by the submarine
                                    operations of Germany and her allies—The question of armed
                                    merchant vessels—The imperiling and destruction of American
                                    lives and property—American demands and German pledges—Relations
                                    of the United States with Germany and her allies</ref>
                                <list>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg143">The continuance of negotiations
                                            concerning the <hi rendition="#italic">Lusitania</hi>
                                            and the <hi rendition="#italic">Ancona</hi>—The sinking
                                            of the <hi rendition="#italic">Persia</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg146">The American proposal of January 18,
                                            1916, of a <hi rendition="#italic">modus Vivendi</hi>
                                            for the observance of rules of international law and
                                            principles of humanity by submarines and the
                                            discontinuance of armament of merchant ships—Further
                                            negotiations concerning the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Lusitania</hi>—The case of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Petrolite</hi>—Reports of
                                            offensive operations by armed merchant ships</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <pb facs="0008" n="IV" xml:id="pgIV"/>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg163">German memorandum of February 10, 1916,
                                            announcing that armed merchant ships are to be treated
                                            as belligerents</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg171">The German Ambassador’s note of
                                            February 16, 1916, on the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Lusitania</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Arabic</hi> cases; his communication of
                                            instructions found on the British armed merchant ship
                                                <hi rendition="#italic">Appam</hi>—Austrian note of
                                            February 22, 1916, on the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Petrolite</hi> incident—Italian note of February
                                            19, 1916, on the <hi rendition="#italic">modus
                                                vivendi</hi> proposal</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg177">The President’s letter of February 24,
                                            1916, asserting the right of American citizens to travel
                                            on armed merchant ships—German memorandum of February
                                            28, 1916, asserting that the pledges of September 1 and
                                            October 5, 1915, do not apply to armed ships</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg185">Defeat in Congress of the resolution to
                                            prevent Americans from traveling on armed merchant
                                            ships—The decision of the German Government against
                                            unlimited submarine warfare—Resolutions in the Reichstag
                                            against restrictions</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg211">Replies of the Allied Governments of
                                            March 23, 1916, to the proposal for a <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">modus vivendi</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg214">The sinking of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi>, March 24,
                                            1916—Cases of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Englishman</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">Manchester
                                                Engineer</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Berwindvale</hi>, and <hi rendition="#italic">Eagle
                                                Point</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg227">The German note of April 10, 1916, on
                                            the <hi rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi> and other
                                            cases</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg232">The American note of April 18, 1916, on
                                            the <hi rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi>, stating that
                                            diplomatic relations would be severed unless the current
                                            methods of submarine warfare were abandoned—Memorandum
                                            published on April 27, 1916, concerning the status of
                                            armed merchant ships—Memorandum communicated to Germany
                                            on April 28, 1916, regarding the conduct of naval
                                            vessels toward enemy and neutral merchant ships</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg257">German reply of May 4, 1916, promising
                                            to observe the rules of visit and search and to provide
                                            for the safety of persons on board destroyed ships—Note
                                            of May 7, 1916, acknowledging responsibility in the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Sussex</hi> case—American note
                                            of May 8, 1916, accepting the German assurances as
                                            unconditional</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg276">The American note of June 21, 1916, on
                                            the <hi rendition="#italic">Petrolite</hi>
                                            incident—Austrian replies of July 17 and September 9,
                                            1916—The ease of the <hi rendition="#italic">Owego</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg291">The renewed struggle in Germany for
                                            unrestricted submarine warfare (October 1916)</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg298">Representations to Germany (October
                                            30–November 18, 1916) concerning the sinking of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Rowanmore</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Marina</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Arabia</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Sebek</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Delto</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Lanao</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Columbian</hi>, and <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Chemung</hi> (December 2,
                                            1916)—Replies and correspondence on these cases—Reports
                                            on political conditions in Germany with reference to
                                            submarine policy—Communications of instructions found on
                                            a French armed merchant ship</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg321">Representations of December 9, 1916,
                                            concerning the sinking of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Trevarrack</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Barbara</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic">Trippel</hi>,
                                                <hi rendition="#italic">Lökken</hi>, <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Palermo</hi>, and <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">John Lambert</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                </list>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg328">Neutral rights as affected by the blockading
                                    measures taken by Great Britain and her allies—Interference with
                                    American trade—The black list—Protests and proposals of
                                    retaliation</ref>
                                <list>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg328">The seizure of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Kankakee</hi>—The black list of
                                            neutral ships</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg337">The Trading with the Enemy (Extension
                                            of Powers) Act (December 23, 1915)—Reservation of the
                                            right to protest against this act as affecting American
                                            interests, January 25, 1916—Reports and correspondence
                                            on interference with neutral trade</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg352">British note of February 16, 1916, in
                                            defense of the Trading with the Enemy (Extension of
                                            Powers) Act—The first “statutory list”, February 29,
                                            1916—Representations in the cases of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Hocking, Genesee</hi>, and <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Kankakee</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg361">British order in council of March 30,
                                            1916, further modifying the Declaration of
                                            London—Protest against this order, April 8, 1916—The <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Zamora</hi> decision asserting
                                            the prevalence of international law over orders in
                                            council</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <pb facs="0009" n="V" xml:id="pgV"/>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg368">British note of April 24, 1916, in
                                            general justification of measures of interference with
                                            neutral trade—The abolition of the distinction between
                                            absolute and conditional contraband</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg392">Protests against the black list of
                                            ships (May 19, 1916) and against interference with goods
                                            consigned to the Netherlands Oversea Trust (June 15,
                                            1916)—Condemnation of the cargo of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Joseph W. Fordney</hi>
                                        </ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg408">British order in council and French
                                            decree of July 7, 1916, withdrawing all recognition of
                                            the Declaration of London and proclaiming other
                                            rules—The inclusion of American firms in the “statutory
                                            list” (July 19, 1916)</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg421">Protest of July 26, 1916, against the
                                            blacklisting of American firms</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg430">Protests against the denial of bunker
                                            coal to American vessels not on the “white list” (cases
                                            of the <hi rendition="#italic">Rizal</hi> and <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Charlton Hall</hi>) and against
                                            interference with Philippine commerce (cases of the <hi
                                                rendition="#italic">Chinese Prince, Kafue</hi>, and
                                                <hi rendition="#italic">Aymeric</hi>)</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg444">Acts of Congress authorizing
                                            retaliation against measures detrimental to American
                                            commerce (September 7 and 8, 1916)—British protest
                                            against these acts</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg446">American protest of September 18, 1916,
                                            against the rules of the order in council of July 7,
                                            1916—The requirement of “certificates of interest” for
                                            goods exported from the Philippine Islands to
                                            territories of the British Empire</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg455">British notes in defense of the black
                                            list and in reply to the American protest of September
                                            18, 1916—Further correspondence regarding restrictions
                                            on bunker coal (case of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Pathfinder</hi>)</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg483">Reservation of American rights in
                                            connection with the abolition of the distinction between
                                            absolute and conditional contraband, November 11,
                                            1916—Refusal of the United States to resort to British
                                            courts for maintenance of national rights—Prize court
                                            decisions upholding the legality of the order in council
                                            of March 11, 1915 (cases of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                                >Stigstad</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic">United
                                                States</hi>)</ref>
                                    </item>
                                </list>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg496">Informal arrangements with Great Britain and
                                    her allies regarding the exportation of American goods to enemy
                                    and neutral European countries</ref>
                                <list>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg496">British “letters of assurance” to
                                            American shippers</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg502">Shipments of fruit</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg506">Shipments of tobacco</ref>
                                    </item>
                                </list>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg523">Informal arrangements regarding the importation
                                    of goods from Germany and Austria-Hungary</ref>
                                <list>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg523">Goods ordered before March 1,
                                            1915</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg534">Importation of sugar-beet seed</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg544">Importation of drugs</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg546">Importation of dyes</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg561">Importation of sodium cyanide</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg566">Importation of porcelain guides for
                                            textile machinery</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg571">Importation of machine knitting
                                            needles</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg580">Importation of hops</ref>
                                    </item>
                                </list>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg584">Arrangements for the regulation of imports made
                                    by European neutral countries</ref>
                                <list>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg584">The Netherlands Oversea Trust</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg588">The Norwegian associations and the
                                            Stavanger Packers’ Import Union</ref>
                                    </item>
                                </list>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg591">Interference with the mails by belligerent
                                    governments</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg630">The removal by belligerent naval authorities of
                                    enemy subjects and military reservists from American ships on
                                    the high seas—Cases of the <hi rendition="#italic">China</hi>.
                                        <hi rendition="#italic">Ausable</hi>, <hi
                                        rendition="#italic">Henry S.</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                                        >Marcus L. Urann</hi>, <hi rendition="#italic"
                                        >Missourian</hi> and <hi rendition="#italic">Frederick
                                        Luckenbach</hi>
                                </ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg679">Departures by belligerent governments and naval
                                    authorities from the established rules for exercise of the right
                                    of visit and search at sea</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg688">Projects of cooperation among the neutral
                                    states in defense of neutral rights</ref>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <pb facs="0010" n="VI" xml:id="pgVI"/>
                    <item>PART III <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Neutral Duties</hi>:<list>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg701">The entrance into and transit through the
                                    United States of active and discharged members of belligerent
                                    armed forces</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg708">The sale of contraband goods—The transfer to
                                    another neutral government of warships built in American yards
                                    for a belligerent government</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg714">Treatment of belligerent warships in American
                                    ports: The escape of members of their interned crews</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg722">Treatment of prize ships in American ports—Case
                                    of the <hi rendition="#italic">Appam</hi>
                                </ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg746">Treatment of belligerent merchant ships which
                                    have taken refuge in American ports</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg749">Treatment of armed merchant ships</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg759">Hovering of belligerent warships in close
                                    proximity to territorial waters</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg765">Treatment of belligerent war and merchant
                                    submarines in neutral ports and waters (case of the <hi
                                        rendition="#italic">Deutschland</hi>): Their operations
                                    beyond territorial waters (case of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                                        >U-53</hi>)</ref>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <item>PART IV <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Other Problems and Responsibilities</hi>:<list>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg787">Regulations regarding passports and the
                                    registration of citizens living abroad</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg795">Status of American consular officers in
                                    belligerent territory occupied by enemy troops</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg796">The proclamation by Germany and Austria-Hungary
                                    creating the Kingdom of Poland</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg798">Appointment of a new Austro-Hungarian
                                    Ambassador</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg807">Questions of diplomatic immunity—Case of Wolf
                                    von Igel </ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg815">Representation of belligerent governments in
                                    enemy countries—Protection of consular property</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg825">The right of asylum—Case of the British Vice
                                    Consul at Sofia</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg829">Protection of American institutions and
                                    citizens in Turkey—Efforts in behalf of other foreigners</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg846">Representations in behalf of Armenians and
                                    Syrians</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg858">Representations in behalf of civilians deported
                                    from northern French cities and from Belgium</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg870">Representations in behalf of Irish political
                                    prisoners</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg871">Correspondence relating to relief work</ref>
                                <list>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg871">Belgian relief</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg886">Polish relief</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg913">Serbian relief</ref>
                                    </item>
                                    <item>
                                        <ref target="#pg924">Relief in Turkish territories</ref>
                                    </item>
                                </list>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg941">The exemption of hospital supplies from seizure
                                    as contraband of war: The question of their shipment to the
                                    Central Empires</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg959">The shipment to the Central Empires of milk for
                                    babies</ref>
                            </item>
                            <item>
                                <ref target="#pg972">The Economic Conference of the Allied
                                    Governments</ref>
                            </item>
                        </list>
                    </item>
                    <item>
                        <ref target="#pg985">
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Index</hi>
                        </ref>
                    </item>
                </list>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
</TEI>
